Now `fetchConversationTree`. Need to map local to `ConversationTree`. Let me write:
```
export async function fetchConversationTree(conversationId: string): Promise<ConversationTree> {
  return withLocal(
    () =>
      getJson<ConversationTree>(`/api/conversations/${encodeURIComponent(conversationId)}/tree`),
    async (bff) => {
      const tree = await bff.memory.conversationTree(conversationId);
      return {
        conversation: tree.conversation,
        messages: tree.messages.map((turn) => ({
          id: String(turn.message_id ?? turn.id),
          parent_id: turn.parent_id ?? null,
          role: turn.role,
          content: turn.content,
          created_at: turn.created_at,
        })),
        head_id: tree.head_id,
      };
    },
  );
}
```
But `withLocal<T>` requires both return `T`. Sidecar returns `Promise<ConversationTree>`, local returns `Promise<ConversationTree>`. Good. `T = ConversationTree`.

But wait — `conversationTree` in `LocalMemoryService` returns `Promise<{ conversation: StoredConversation; messages: StoredTurn[]; head_id: string | null }>`. `StoredConversation` extends `Conversation`, so `conversation` is assignable to `Conversation`. Good.

Now `setConversationHead`: sidecar returns `{ conversation: Conversation }`, local returns `StoredConversation` (extends Conversation). Need `T = Conversation`:
```
return withLocal(
  () => sendJson<{ conversation: Conversation }>(...).then(b => b.conversation),
  async (bff) => bff.memory.setConversationHead(conversationId, headId),
);
```
`setConversationHead` returns `Promise<StoredConversation>` which is assignable to `Promise<Conversation>`. Good.

`createConversation`: sidecar returns `{ conversation: Conversation }`, local returns `Conversation`. Need `T = Conversation`:
```
() => postJson<{ conversation: Conversation }>("/api/conversations", {}).then(b => b.conversation),
async (bff) => bff.memory.createConversation(),
```

Let me apply these.

