The issue is "unavailable" text is broken up because the value cell contains `{capability.status === "available" ? "available" : "unavailable"}` followed by `{capability.reason ? ` — ${capability.reason}` : ""}`. Since reason is "Sidecar unreachable.", the cell text is "unavailable — Sidecar unreachable." which is split across two elements (the status text and the reason text). So `getByText("unavailable")` fails because the text node isn't exactly "unavailable".

Let me change the assertion to use regex `/unavailable/` or check the row contains "unavailable". Let me use `screen.getByText(/^unavailable$/)` won't work because of the reason appended. Let me just check the row contains both the label and "unavailable".

Let me rewrite the assertions to query rows:
```tsx
const llmRow = screen.getByText(/sidecar model proxy/i).closest("li");
expect(llmRow).toHaveTextContent(/unavailable/);
const dataRow = screen.getByText(/local canvas data/i).closest("li");
expect(dataRow).toHaveTextContent("available");
```

`toHaveTextContent("available")` exact might fail if there's extra whitespace but should be fine since data row cell is exactly "available". Actually toHaveTextContent matches substring by default? No, `toHaveTextContent(text)` matches if the element's text content equals the string (normalized). For "available" it's exact. For "unavailable — Sidecar unreachable." I'll use regex.

